The **Plugin** node runs a third-party audio plugin inside your pipeline. Splitwave hosts **CLAP**, **VST3** and, on macOS, **Audio Unit** plugins. Pick one from the list in the node; use **Rescan** if you just installed it.

The pipeline has to be running for the plugin to load, so the plugin window button works only while it runs.

## Sockets follow the plugin

When you pick a plugin, Splitwave asks it which audio inputs and outputs it has and builds the node from that answer. The plugin decides, not the node.

- A plugin with a **sidechain input** gets an extra socket on the node for it, named `sidechain1`, `sidechain2` and so on, in the order the plugin lists them.
- An input that nothing is connected to stays switched off in the plugin, so a plugin that misbehaves with an unused sidechain is left alone.
- If Splitwave cannot read the plugin's ports, the node shows a red message and stays silent. It does not guess a layout.

## Wide cables and copies

A cable can carry more channels than a plugin takes. A stereo plugin on a 6 channel cable is the usual case. Splitwave handles it in this order:

1. If the cable is wider than the plugin's own width, the plugin is first asked to take the **whole width** as one instance. VST3 and Audio Unit plugins that can do this run as a single instance. CLAP plugins have a fixed width and are never widened.
2. If the plugin refuses, and **Copy per channel group** is on, Splitwave runs **one copy of the plugin for each group of channels**: channels 1-2 go to the first copy, 3-4 to the second, and so on. Every channel is processed. A badge such as **x3** in the node's header shows how many copies are running.
3. All copies start from the same settings, and the plugin window edits the first copy. Their delay is matched, so the groups stay in time with each other.

**Copy per channel group** is **on** by default. It is available only when the plugin has the same number of channels in and out. A plugin that changes the channel count always runs as one instance.

## Channels that pass through

Turn **Copy per channel group** off and one instance handles only the channels it has. Every channel beyond that goes **through the node untouched**, as in a DAW when a stereo plugin sits on a wider track.

Those channels are marked on the node: the socket is faded and a small arrow sits beside it, on the inner side of the node.

<img src={socket} alt="A pass-through socket: faded, with an arrow beside it" />

In the node below, the Duck plugin takes two channels. Channels 3 to 5 are faded with arrows, and they leave the node as they came in.

<img src={node} alt="A stereo plugin with Copy per channel group off, on a 5 channel cable" />

If the plugin takes **more** channels in than it gives out, the extra input channels are consumed by it and leave silent.

